# win32u: Measure the glyphs of fonts with embedded bitmaps like they are drawn.
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12797 state=opened
# Monochrome text is drawn with the advance widths of the embedded bitmaps, but GetTextExtentPoint32(), GetCharWidth32() and GGO_METRICS measured the hinted outlines, so the measured width of a string differed from the drawn one. Windows takes the advance widths from the embedded bitmaps at the sizes that have them, regardless of the antialiasing, and measures monochrome text with the monochrome hinting.

d3dx9 took the glyph cells of ID3DXFont from GGO_GRAY8_BITMAP, which then no longer matches GGO_METRICS at these sizes. The first commit sizes the cells from GGO_METRICS with the one pixel border the d3dx9 tests expect, and places the antialiased bitmap relative to the glyph origin.

Tahoma Bold 9-13 pixels, no antialiasing; the blue bar is the GetTextExtentPoint32() width, the text is drawn with ExtTextOut():

![win32u-strike-metrics-compare-en-v2](/uploads/c3c585c2b7f35fc869df4fd1ae1834b7/win32u-strike-metrics-compare-en-v2.png)

Wine-Bug: https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=60741
